      "writtenOn": "2021-03-11T13:26:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes. Immediately this was fine because it doesn\u0027t matter what user manager we speak to, the only requirement from the test is that it says it\u0027s a managed profile (and this is only called for the relevant tests, so in practice it\u0027s not *too* bad).\n\nBut yeah it\u0027s setting up a lie across the board, and it\u0027s completely unrealistic (all profiles will declare themselves as work profiles) if we ever try to use this in a non-trivial way.\n\nThis has come up a number of times in Paul\u0027s code and here is what I came up with to solve it, which you can observe in a number of our tests already (here from NetworkNotificationManagerTest) :\n\n  final Context asUserCtx \u003d mock(Context.class, AdditionalAnswers.delegatesTo(mCtx));\n  doReturn(UserHandle.ALL).when(asUserCtx).getUser();\n  when(mCtx.createContextAsUser(eq(UserHandle.ALL), anyInt())).thenReturn(asUserCtx);\n  when(mCtx.getSystemService(eq(Context.NOTIFICATION_SERVICE)))\n        .thenReturn(mNotificationManager);\n\nI like this model a little bit better for its simplicity but it can\u0027t be applied here as is because mServiceContext is not an actual mock, so I went with your approach and an additional function to set whether WP for a given user.",
      "parentUuid": "7c4c37c8_2225239b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 10993,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 10993,
        "endChar": 61
      },
